      "math_academy_id": "e-14146",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{2}{4x+12}=\\dfrac{2}{x+3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\dfrac{2}{4x+12} &= \\dfrac{2}{x+3} \\\t\n\t2 \\cdot (x+3) &= (4x+12) \\cdot 2 \\\n\t2x + 6 &= 8x + 24\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t2x + 6 &= 8x + 24 \\\n\t2x + 6-24 &= 8x + 24-24 \\\n\t2x-18 &= 8x \\\n\t2x-18-2x &= 8x-2x \\\n\t-18 &= 6x \\\n        \\dfrac{-18}{6} &= \\dfrac{6x}{6} \\\n        -3 &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-3$ cannot be a solution, the equation has **no solutions.**",
